			<item>
				<title>how to do literate programming for the web</title>
				<link>/braindump/posts/how_to_do_literate_programming_for_the_web/</link>
				<pubDate>Thu, 11 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0200</pubDate>
				<guid>/braindump/posts/how_to_do_literate_programming_for_the_web/</guid>
				<description>&lt;div class=&#34;ox-hugo-toc toc&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;heading&#34;&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#311ce157-1c91-4270-9ed1-835771c0f639&#34;&gt;Show the element itself&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#48de5094-46aa-420a-b922-2b080589765c&#34;&gt;CSS renders to a picture, so show the picture&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#f1a7bdbb-114d-4229-bbf3-e1d5c280c48b&#34;&gt;Frame a live component beside the prose that builds it&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#7beb8c85-c036-4ffb-9085-49d4be70c803&#34;&gt;Animate the interaction&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#permalink&#34;&gt;Permalink&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;!--endtoc--&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;311ce157-1c91-4270-9ed1-835771c0f639&#34;&gt;Show the element itself&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The general guide says a diagram beats a paragraph wherever the content is&#xA;visual. On the web the bar is higher still: the content is not merely&#xA;visual, it is the live artifact itself, and the export can hold it. So&#xA;whenever the prose introduces something the reader will see or touch — a&#xA;styled element, a widget, a page fragment — show that thing, rendered, right&#xA;there.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
